<Page
  title="superloops: the metamodel"
  description="Every change path designed as a set of validation loops: sensors, tools, loops and the metaloop that improves them."
>
  <header class="title-block">
    <h1>Metamodel</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      We believe every change path should be designed as a set of validation loops. Some loops are short and fast,
      like running unit tests on a change. Others take longer, like running end-to-end tests. Inner loops run while
      the work is being done, before anything is pushed to git. Outer loops run later: in CI, on staging, or even after
      the code has reached real users, like scanning production logs.
    </p>
  </header>

  <figure class="figure">
    <ChangePath />
    <figcaption>
      <span class="figure-number">Figure 1.</span> A change path with its inner and outer loops. Each loop reads its
      sensor at one point on the path and sends a failure back to the work; the later the point, the longer the loop
      and the more the failure costs.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A validation loop judges the quality of a change by reading a sensor, and improves the change when the sensor
      spots a problem.
    </p>
    <p>
      A successful AI-first development process is built around designing the right loops: loops that do the checking
      for you. The right set of loops is different for every project and depends on its needs and constraints. It is
      always a trade-off. A loop costs time, money and attention, so it pays off only where the failure it catches is
      likely or expensive.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sensor">Sensor</h2>
    <p>
      A sensor is anything that observes a change, or the system the change lives in, and reports a signal about its
      quality: pass or fail, a score, or a list of findings. A sensor only observes. It never changes the work.
    </p>
    <p>Sensors come in three kinds:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Computational</strong>: a program that gives the same answer every time. It is cheap and fast, but it
        sees only what it was written to see.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Inferential</strong>: a model reads the work and judges it. It can see what no fixed rule can express,
        but it costs money, it can be wrong, and it has to be calibrated before it is trusted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Human</strong>: a person looks at the result or answers a question. This is the slowest and most
        expensive kind, and the final judge of whether the result is what was wanted.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Examples:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A test runner: the tests pass or fail on the changed code.</li>
      <li>A linter whose message says how to fix the problem.</li>
      <li>An LLM reviewer that reads the diff with a fresh context and lists what it finds.</li>
      <li>A service’s error rate after a release, compared with the rate before it.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="tool">Tool</h2>
    <p>
      A tool is the concrete program or service a sensor is built with. The sensor is what is measured; the tool is
      how. One sensor can be built with different tools, and one tool can serve several sensors. Choosing the sensors
      is a design decision. Choosing the tools depends on the project’s stack.
    </p>
    <p>Examples:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The test-runner sensor is pytest in a Python project, Jest in a TypeScript one, and go test in Go.</li>
      <li>
        Playwright serves two sensors: it drives a browser through a user flow, and it takes screenshots of the result.
      </li>
      <li>Production logs are read with LogQL in Loki, and the errors users actually hit come from Sentry.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="loop">Loop</h2>
    <p>A loop connects a sensor to the work. It defines three things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>When</strong> the sensor is read: on every edit, before the agent stops, on every pull request, every
        night, or after a release.
      </li>
      <li><strong>What</strong> counts as a failure.</li>
      <li><strong>What happens next</strong>: who fixes the work, and when to stop and escalate to a person.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The loop repeats until the sensor passes, or until it gives up and asks for help. Where a loop sits in the change
      path decides how early a problem is caught and how much it costs to fix.
    </p>
    <p>Examples:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Green before stop</strong> (inner, local): the agent can’t end its turn while the tests or the linter
        fail. It reads the failure, fixes the code, and runs them again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Pull request review</strong> (before merge): an LLM reviewer reads the diff. A blocking finding sends
        the change back to the agent, and a person settles any dispute.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Production log scan</strong> (outer, after release): after a deploy, the error signatures in the logs
        are compared with those from before it. A new signature opens an issue or triggers a rollback.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="metaloop">Metaloop</h2>
    <p>The loops themselves are improved in a loop:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Do the task.</li>
      <li>Notice which sensors or loops should have caught a problem (or caught it earlier), and improve them.</li>
      <li>Repeat.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      For example, a reviewer finds the same mistake in a third pull request. Instead of fixing it a third time, add a
      lint rule or a test that catches it before review, at the earliest phase that can see it.
    </p>
  </div>
